# Review: FileSystemPlugin

The core loop is solid: the open operation shows a native picker, scans markdown files, and registers
or replaces a workspace by path. Each markdown file is bound to an ECHO `Text` object in a
per-workspace mirror space. Edits are auto-saved back to disk, and watchers on the directory and on
each file pull external changes back in. The data types match the spec field for field.

What is missing is mostly the user-facing surface the spec describes. No "Close Folder" or
"Refresh Folder" action exists in the navtree. Close is reachable only from the "Remove folder"
button in the General settings article, and `RefreshDirectory` is never invoked anywhere; refresh
happens only through the directory watcher. The settings form edits icon and hue, not the name, so
rename (F-6.3) does not exist.

The biggest cross-cutting divergence is the platform. The spec intro (and `dx.config.ts`) says the
plugin needs the Electron-based desktop app, but every disk call goes through Tauri
(`@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog`, `@tauri-apps/plugin-fs`, the `get_xattr`/`set_xattr` commands). Composer
registers the plugin only when `isTauri && !isMobile && !isPopover`
(`packages/apps/composer-app/src/plugin-defs.tsx`). The spec also contradicts itself on images: the
intro and F-1.3 say images are surfaced and scanned, while F-4.5 says they are ignored. The code
ignores them; `SUPPORTED_IMAGE_EXTENSIONS` is declared but unused.

Other notes:

- Workspace persistence is in browser `localforage`, not ECHO, though `closeDirectory` declares
  `echo:write`.
- Opening a folder writes to disk (`.composer/meta.json`, `.composer/filemap.json`, and an
  `org.dxos.dxn` xattr on every markdown file).
- Closing a workspace leaves its mirror ECHO space behind.
- Of 40 items: 8 done, 13 untested, 10 partial, 3 diverged, 6 missing. Two test files exercise spec items:
  `app-graph-builder.test.ts` covers the data types, nested-directory expansion (F-4.2) and graph sync on state
  replacement (part of F-3.1), and composer-app's `file-system.test.ts` covers the dev route's path rules for the
  `FileSystem` service. `plugin.test.ts` builds the plugin with all seven modules but checks only that
  `OperationHandler` activates. Nothing tests open, close, refresh, save-to-disk, ordering or settings.
- The `FileSystem` service matches its interface on both backends (Tauri fs and the dev route `/__dx/fs`) and now
  also carries an optional `git` member the spec does not list. Only the dev route's root and `.mdl` rules are tested;
  plugin-code's `sync.test.ts` replaces the client with an in-memory stand-in. As its note says, the plugin's own
  workspace code does not use it yet.
- No scenario or QA test is exercised end to end. T-1 and T-4 are partly exercised: the graph test asserts the
  navtree shape that their `then:` clauses describe, from seeded state. T-2, T-3, T-5, T-6 and QA-1 are run by
  nothing (QA-1 is blocked to agents, since the plugin loads only under Tauri).

